		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>How they differ</h2>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Different products for different audiences</h3>
		<p>
			Mail-in-a-Box's README is explicit: <em>"no user-configurable setup options."</em>
			That's the value proposition. The project deliberately optimises
			for a one-command personal email box over flexibility — a deliberately narrow scope.
			Vectis Mail is built for the wider job: production infrastructure you can operate,
			automate, and scale across domains and teams.
		</p>
		<p>
			Vectis Mail sits in a different category. The audience is teams, SaaS, and multi-domain
			operators who need a real transactional sending API, inbound webhooks,
			multi-domain hosting, and config-as-code. Comparing the two on the same table is useful for
			the migration question, but understand what each product is for.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">API surface</h3>
		<p>
			Mail-in-a-Box exposes a minimal REST API for control-panel operations: creating users,
			managing aliases, setting forwards. There's no transactional sending API, no inbound
			webhooks, no domain-scoped keys, no per-domain rate limiting. SMTP submission is the
			only programmatic send path.
		</p>
		<p>
			That matters the day an application, not a person, needs to send. On Mail-in-a-Box your
			app logs in over SMTP with a real mailbox's password, so every service that sends mail
			holds a user credential, and a leaked one opens the whole mailbox. On Vectis Mail each
			app gets its own API key, scoped to the domains it may send from and with its own rate
			limit, and it posts JSON (single or batch, with attachments) instead of speaking SMTP.
			Replies and inbound mail can come back to your app as HMAC-signed webhooks with retry,
			which Mail-in-a-Box has no equivalent for. In all, 40+ REST endpoints, also covering
			mailboxes, aliases, domains, analytics and audit logs.
		</p>
		<p>
			See the <a href="/api/">API Reference</a> for the full surface.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Multi-domain hosting</h3>
		<p>
			Mail-in-a-Box is single-org-shaped. You can run multiple domains on one box, but the
			control panel, the user model, and the operational shape all assume one organisation.
			Vectis Mail hosts many domains on one install, each isolated at the domain level —
			its own DKIM keys, analytics, and spam controls. If you're running mail for
			multiple brands or customer subdomains, that's a structural difference, not a config tweak.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Configuration model</h3>
		<p>
			Mail-in-a-Box's configuration lives in the web control panel and on-disk files managed
			by the install script. There's no declarative config surface. Every change is a UI
			click or a manual file edit, and the upgrade path documented as <em>"fresh OS only"</em>
			means modifying the box voids the supported upgrade story.
		</p>
		<p>
			Vectis Mail takes the opposite position to "no user-configurable setup options", without
			asking you to hand-edit Postfix. Day-to-day changes (domains, mailboxes, aliases, API keys)
			happen in the admin dashboard or over the API and apply at once. The settings Mail-in-a-Box
			won't let you touch, such as spam thresholds, which services run and resource limits, sit
			in one file the installer writes for you, and <code>vectis config apply</code> shows the
			diff, then regenerates and reloads only the services that changed. Settings made this way
			are carried through every update rather than fighting it.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Update + rollback</h3>
		<p>
			Mail-in-a-Box upgrades by re-running the setup script: straightforward when the box
			is unmodified, fragile when you've customised anything. There's no automatic snapshot,
			no health-check gate, no rollback. If a release breaks something, recovery is whatever
			you do manually.
		</p>
		<p>
			On Vectis Mail an update is one command, <code>vectis update apply</code>, and it is
			the same whether or not you've customised the box. It snapshots the database and
			stack first, pulls the new release's images (verified against a signed manifest),
			recreates the services, and checks that Postfix, Dovecot, Rspamd and the API are all
			answering before it calls the update done. If any step fails, it restores the
			snapshot on its own. You never find out mid-upgrade
			that a hand edit broke the setup script, and <code>vectis verify</code> confirms
			afterwards that the box runs exactly the published release. See
			<a href="/getting-started/installation/">Installation</a> for the full update model.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Calendar + contacts</h3>
		<p>
			Mail-in-a-Box bundles Nextcloud for CalDAV and CardDAV; calendar and contacts work
			out of the box. Vectis Mail doesn't ship calendar/contacts yet; that's on the Phase 4
			roadmap. If you actively use CalDAV/CardDAV, that's our most significant functional
			gap today — plan to pair Vectis Mail with a separate CalDAV/CardDAV service until
			Phase 4 lands.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">License + pricing</h3>
		<p>
			Mail-in-a-Box is CC0-1.0: public domain, the most permissive license possible. Zero
			lock-in, zero strings, no commercial edition, no paid support, donations welcome.
		</p>
		<p>
			Vectis Mail is dual-licensed: BSL 1.1 for the core (free Starter: up to 3 domains,
			25 mailboxes per domain, full API, webhooks, monitoring, backups) and a commercial
			Pro license at $39 USD/tenant/month (unlimited domains and mailboxes, per-domain analytics,
			OIDC SSO, priority support, one subscription covers unlimited installs). See
			<a href="/pricing/">pricing</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Migrating from Mail-in-a-Box to Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p>
			The mail-data layer migrates cleanly (both products are vanilla Postfix + Dovecot).
			The calendar/contacts and control-panel-config layers need manual export.
		</p>
		<ol class="alt-steps">
			<li><strong>Check who serves your DNS.</strong> Mail-in-a-Box runs its own DNS server (nsd4) and many boxes are their domain's nameserver. If yours is, move DNS hosting to your registrar or a DNS provider first, copying every record across, because the old box is about to go away.</li>
			<li><strong>Take a final Mail-in-a-Box backup</strong> (its built-in duplicity backup), so you have a restore point that doesn't depend on the migration.</li>
			<li><strong>Stand up Vectis Mail</strong> on a fresh Ubuntu 24.04 VPS with the <a href="/getting-started/installation/">installation guide</a>, add your domains, and leave MX pointing at Mail-in-a-Box for now.</li>
			<li><strong>Copy the mailboxes</strong> with <code>imapsync</code> or <code>doveadm backup</code>. Both products use Dovecot, so folders and flags come across intact.</li>
			<li><strong>Publish Vectis Mail's DKIM key</strong> next to the existing one. Mail-in-a-Box signs with the selector <code>mail</code> and Vectis Mail with <code>default</code>, so both keys stay valid through the switchover.</li>
			<li><strong>Export Roundcube settings</strong> (per-user filters, signatures) from Mail-in-a-Box Roundcube and re-import into Vectis Mail Roundcube.</li>
			<li><strong>Re-create domains, mailboxes, aliases, and forwards</strong> via Vectis Mail's CLI or API. The Mail-in-a-Box control panel doesn't export config-as-code, so this is a manual transcription.</li>
			<li><strong>Export Nextcloud calendar + contacts</strong> to iCal/vCard if you use them. Vectis Mail can't import these — you'll need a separate CalDAV/CardDAV destination from this point. This is the most significant step if calendar is part of your daily use.</li>
			<li><strong>Switch MX and SPF</strong> to the Vectis Mail server, run a final <code>imapsync</code> pass for anything that arrived meanwhile, and point mail clients at the new host.</li>
			<li><strong>Keep the old box for a week</strong>, read-only, then switch it off once mail has been flowing cleanly through the new server.</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Realistic timeline: an afternoon for a small personal install without calendar,
			longer if Nextcloud calendar/contacts are central to your use.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
		<div class="alt-faq">
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is Vectis Mail a drop-in replacement for Mail-in-a-Box?</h3>
				<p>
					Not 1:1 — the two products target different scopes. Mail-in-a-Box is a
					single-server, single-org, one-command personal email box. Vectis Mail is
					API-first, multi-domain mail + transactional sending built for teams and SaaS
					running production infrastructure. The mail-data layer (Postfix + Dovecot)
					migrates cleanly via standard IMAP sync, so the switch is a normal migration
					rather than an export fight. Once you need a real REST API, inbound webhooks,
					or genuine multi-domain hosting, Vectis Mail is the natural next step.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does Mail-in-a-Box have a sending API or inbound webhooks?</h3>
				<p>
					Mail-in-a-Box exposes a minimal REST API for control-panel operations (creating
					users, managing aliases) but not a transactional sending API and not inbound
					webhooks. SMTP submission with mailbox credentials is the only programmatic send
					path, and inbound mail lands in mailboxes only. Vectis Mail ships 40+ REST
					endpoints covering sending (single + batch with attachments), inbound webhooks
					with HMAC signatures, mailboxes, domains, analytics, and admin operations.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Can I run Mail-in-a-Box on something other than Ubuntu 22.04?</h3>
				<p>
					No — Mail-in-a-Box explicitly targets fresh Ubuntu 22.04 LTS only. No Debian,
					no Ubuntu 24.04, no RHEL family, no BSD. Modifying the install or running it on
					anything else voids the upgrade path. Vectis Mail runs as a Docker Compose stack
					on a current Linux VPS, and it is tested on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, the release our own
					production servers run.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Will I lose my calendar and contacts if I migrate?</h3>
				<p>
					Calendar and contacts on Mail-in-a-Box live in its bundled Nextcloud install
					(CalDAV/CardDAV). Vectis Mail doesn't yet ship CalDAV/CardDAV (it's on the
					Phase 4 roadmap), so you'd export to iCal/vCard before tearing the old box
					down and re-import wherever you keep calendar from then on. If calendar and
					contacts are central to your use, that's a real cost to factor into your
					migration plan.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

